Root on scubed3 (initramfs-tools, Debian and derivatives)
=========================================================

The root file system is on the scubed3 partition "root", best with LUKS on
it (see "LUKS on top of a partition" in the main README), and its keys are in
the key store /etc/scubed3.keys (see "Key store" in the main README). The
initramfs starts the daemon, asks one passphrase, as for an encrypted disk,
opens the partitions, switches the paranoia level and sets up root as a
block device; the daemon keeps running after the switch to the real root,
until the computer is switched off.

Files:

	hooks/scubed3			-> /etc/initramfs-tools/hooks/scubed3
	scripts/local-top/scubed3	-> /etc/initramfs-tools/scripts/local-top/scubed3
	initramfs.conf.example		-> /etc/scubed3/initramfs.conf

What happens at boot (scripts/local-top/scubed3):

1. scubed3 is started on BASE, with its mount point /dev/scubed3fs and its
   control socket /run/scubed3/control (/run/scubed3/fs is a link to
   /dev/scubed3fs, for /etc/crypttab of the real root, for a LUKS volume
   whose key is not in the key store)
2. the passphrase is asked as cryptroot (cryptsetup-initramfs) asks it for
   an encrypted disk, with its askpass (the console, plymouth, also
   cryptroot-unlock over ssh), with the same messages:

	Please unlock disk vda3_crypt:
	cryptsetup: ERROR: vda3_crypt: cryptsetup failed, bad password or options?
	cryptsetup: vda3_crypt: set up successfully

   vda3_crypt is CRYPTTAB_NAME, by default as the debian-installer names
   an encrypted partition: the name of the base device and _crypt. Nothing
   else is shown on the console (the messages of scubed3 go to
   /run/initramfs/scubed3.log): someone who looks at the boot sees what an
   encrypted disk looks like.

   The passphrases are passphrases of the key store, they are asked until
   one has a view with "root". A passphrase of another view (with hidden
   partitions) unlocks that view, and gets the message of a wrong
   passphrase: type the hidden passphrases first, then the one of root.
   After TRIES passphrases without root (default 3, 0: no limit; the
   hidden ones count) the message of cryptroot, "maximum number of tries
   exceeded", and the boot goes on without a root device, as with
   cryptroot (the initramfs drops to its shell when root does not appear)
3. all partitions of the unlocked views are opened (in paranoia level 2
   and 3 ALL partitions must be open: all of them must be in the views that
   are unlocked at boot), and the LUKS volumes of the view of root, with
   their keys from the key store, as /dev/mapper/scubed3-NAME (other file
   systems, /home, ..., are mounted from /etc/fstab); the LUKS volumes of
   the hidden views are not opened; the copy of the key store in the
   initramfs is overwritten with random data and removed
4. the paranoia level is switched (PARANOIA in initramfs.conf, otherwise
   paranoia= in /etc/scubed3.conf, otherwise 2); this is before anything is
   written
5. root becomes a block device: /dev/mapper/scubed3-root if it has LUKS on
   it (otherwise a loop device); ROOT is set to it (root= on the kernel
   command line, for example root=UUID=... as grub writes it, is replaced)

After the boot, scubed3ctl works as usual (the socket is the default one).

The hook copies /etc/scubed3.keys into the initramfs (it is random data
without its passphrases), and askpass of cryptsetup-initramfs; it fails
without the key store (the old initramfs is kept). After a change of the
key store, run update-initramfs -u. ROOT_PARTITION (of an older version) is
not used anymore: root is always the partition root of the key store; to
boot a partition with another name, give it the name root in the key store
(scubed3ctl: keys-copy NAME root, then keys-remove NAME).


Installation
~~~~~~~~~~~~

1. Install scubed3 (make install, or the package scubed3, see
   contrib/debian-installer/README; the debian-installer can do all of
   this, with partman-scubed3), and cryptsetup and cryptsetup-initramfs
   for LUKS on the root partition; set CRYPTSETUP=y in
   /etc/cryptsetup-initramfs/conf-hook, so that cryptsetup-initramfs adds
   cryptsetup with the crypto modules and the device mapper rules to the
   initramfs (there is no /etc/crypttab entry for it).

2. Make a key store with a view with root (and the other partitions that
   are opened at boot), make root with LUKS on it (its keys from the key
   store) and copy the system into it, for example (from the running
   system, or from a live system):

	# scubed3ctl
	s3> keys-setup		(a passphrase, the partition root in its view;
				 another passphrase for hidden partitions)
	s3> unlock-all
	s3> create root
	s3> resize root 2000
	s3> luksformat root
	s3> mke2fs root
	s3> mount root /mnt/newroot
	# rsync -aHAXx / /mnt/newroot/

   An existing partition with another name: keys-import NAME (its
   passphrase, and of its LUKS volume), keys-copy NAME root.

   and in the new system: /etc/fstab with the root file system
   (UUID=... of the file system in the partition), /boot on a normal
   (unencrypted) partition, no swap on scubed3 (see Limitations).

3. Copy the three files (see above, the scripts executable) in the new
   system, set BASE (a name that does not change: /dev/disk/by-partuuid/...
   or /dev/disk/by-id/...) and OPTIONS (the same -m and -M as always) in
   /etc/scubed3/initramfs.conf, and copy /etc/scubed3.conf (the salt and
   the iterations) and the key store /etc/scubed3.keys (mode 0600). Then,
   chrooted in the new system:

	# update-initramfs -u -k all
	# update-grub

   The hook fails if scubed3, scubed3ctl, losetup or the key store is
   missing, so that the old initramfs is kept.

4. Do not start scubed3 on the base device in the new system (no service
   for it): it already runs. Do not close the root partition, and do not
   close any partition while you want to stay in level 2 or 3 (closing
   switches back to level 1).

The keyboard layout for the passphrase: KEYMAP=y in
/etc/initramfs-tools/initramfs.conf (with console-setup). With a splash
screen (plymouth) the passphrase is asked by plymouth, as for an encrypted
disk.


Shutdown
~~~~~~~~

The root file system depends on the daemon until the end:

- the daemon runs as @scubed3: systemd does not kill processes whose
  argv[0] starts with @ when the system is shut down (storage daemons for
  the root file system, see "Storage Daemons for the Root File System" in
  the documentation of systemd); the initramfs sets its oom_score_adj to
  -1000, the OOM killer does not kill it
- its mount point is below /dev: systemd-shutdown does not unmount file
  systems below /dev, /sys and /proc; it unmounts the other ones with
  MNT_FORCE, for FUSE that aborts the connection, the root file system
  would lose its device before it is remounted read-only
- when the root file system is remounted read-only it is flushed: the loop
  device (or dm-crypt on it) sends the flush to the partition, scubed3
  writes its current block (as with fsync)
- the daemon never opens a file by its path after its start (the base
  device is opened once, random data comes from getrandom(2)), so it keeps
  working after the files of the initramfs are deleted (dev/rootswitchtest.py
  tests this)

In paranoia level 3 the daemon keeps writing until the power is off; a write
that is interrupted by that is ignored at the next start (see Durability in
the main README).


Limitations
~~~~~~~~~~~

- The boot partition is not hidden: the initramfs shows that scubed3 is
  used and which device is the base device (the threat model assumes that
  anyway); the boot only looks like that of an encrypted disk. The key
  store is in it (random data without its passphrases).
- One root: the partition root of the view of the passphrase that is
  typed last. Two views with a root with other keys (one you can admit, a
  hidden one) cannot both be unlocked at boot (the name is ambiguous, the
  boot goes on without root).
- No swap on scubed3, and avoid running out of memory: the kernel writes
  dirty pages of the root file system through the daemon, which needs memory
  to do that (a known limit of root file systems on FUSE). Swap on zram, or
  no swap.
- In paranoia level 3 the rate limits the root file system too: give the
  root partition enough blocks, or a higher rate (see Paranoia levels in
  the main README).
- The KDF of the key store (PBKDF2, iterations of /etc/scubed3.conf) takes
  some seconds per passphrase, probably longer than that of a LUKS volume.
- This was tested in a simulated initramfs (dev/initramfstest.sh: the
  local-top script with stand-ins for the initramfs-tools functions), not
  in a real boot; please report problems.
